\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a finite group $G$, write $d(G)$ for its minimal number of generators, and $[G:H]=|G|/|H|$ for a subgroup index. A family of indices has no common divisor when its greatest common divisor is $1$. All subgroups are ordinary abstract subgroups and no normality is required.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every finite group $G$ and integer $d\geq0$, if there are subgroups $H_1,\ldots,H_m\leq G$ with $d(H_i)\leq d$ for every $i$ and
\[
 \gcd([G:H_1],\ldots,[G:H_m])=1,
\]
must $d(G)\leq d+1$? \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
If a finite group has d-generator subgroups with relatively prime indices collectively, must the whole group have at most d+1 generators?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2596 (KOU-21.87). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] A. Lucchini, Problem 21.87, in E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), \emph{The Kourovka Notebook: Unsolved Problems in Group Theory}, 21st edition, arXiv:1401.0300v48 (6 October 2026). This version records an affirmative answer; the displayed question is the historically registered target. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}.
\end{description}
